WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
Developed from the popular cartoon series, Spy vs.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.760
Spy is Mad Magazine's first official computer game.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:29.460
And welcome to NEStalgia, chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.720
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.540
And I'm Joe. Guys, is it wrong to call this a computer game?

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:45.900
Well, Mike, this was actually a game for the Commodore 64 before it came to the Nintendo Entertainment System.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:51.800
And that's true, and you know I know that because I always have the sequels and spinoffs section towards the end of the show.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:55.880
But I guess what I mean is, I'm just reading the back of the NES boxes.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.700
So do you think they just got lazy and didn't change what they were talking about when they put it on the back of the NES?

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:05.780
I think if you want to get like really technical, you can say it's a computer game.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:09.620
Like it's like something like my grandparents would call an NES game, but like they're not wrong.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:11.740
I mean, the NES is a computer technically.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:20.520
So like, I feel like, yeah, they're not wrong, but they probably also were just like didn't change the description from the from the other version.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:24.880
Yeah. And Nintendo doesn't refer to it as a computer, the NES.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.960
They refer to it as the control deck.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.340
So this is Mad Magazine's first official control deck game.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.400
Is that is that how we're going to go about it?

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:40.000
I think that we were only we only saw control deck referenced in one manual, right?

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.920
Yeah. You know, control deck that I don't know.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:44.740
I feel like I have seen that elsewhere.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.020
But yeah, it's it's strictly a technical term.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.900
It's not going to go anywhere. Anyway, this whole conversation has been silly.

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:51.220
And you know what else is silly?

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:52.740
Mad Magazine.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:58.200
Oh, did you guys read Mad Magazine?

00:01:58.540 --> 00:01:59.840
Yeah, I never read it.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:00.940
I never read it.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:02.420
So, Sean, how about you?

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:13.610
I think I had, you know, to get them at the library, because I had a really, a really big old magazine section.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.320
And they were fun.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:26.620
They were just like, you know, little satirical things, mostly, you know, insensitive humor about a lot of things that probably wouldn't fly now.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:27.940
But apparently it's still running.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:31.560
So I'd like to take a look at a more recent copy.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:41.960
Yeah. You know, what's interesting is, like, I looked at just the issue that Spy vs Spy premiered in just to see, like, oh, what is what is a Mad Magazine?

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.580
And what is Spy vs Spy in a cartoon format?

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:54.280
And what was weird about that particular issue was, one, it was very political because it happened to be the election issue for John F.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.340
Kennedy and Nixon.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:58.440
So I thought that was interesting.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:04.920
But then, like, it also had this thing where if you read it one way, it acts as if Richard Nixon won the election.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.000
But if you read it backwards, John F.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:08.120
Kennedy won the election.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:11.040
Well, they were, they were clever like that.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:20.080
I always, I always thought that they had some really funny, like, gimmicks in their, in their magazines.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:23.160
And they never really dropped the political part.

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:26.760
Like, I remember seeing one about the 2000 election.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.480
So that, yeah, that's just sort of their bag.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:31.600
I guess I didn't realize that it was that long running.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:35.280
I didn't know it was, it was, you know, from what, the 60s?

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:41.080
Yeah, I've only seen, I've only read copies from like the 90s, like late 90s and early 2000s.

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:47.380
But, yeah, I mean, I'm looking at all the covers from across the history.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:48.960
And, yeah, there's a lot of them.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:54.960
I mean, even just to prove how little research I did for mad, doesn't that kid have a name?

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:56.040
The kid who's on every cover?

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:57.900
Yeah, that's Alfred E.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:00.160
Newman. Why did I think it was like Howdy Doody?

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.540
Because it kind of looks like a Howdy Doody.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:08.560
Okay. You see, I grew up with Nickelodeon Magazine.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:14.440
And I feel like that's probably on some respect similar or like an offshoot of Mad Magazine.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:16.600
Like that's where they got their influence from.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:25.600
Because that whole thing was filled with mostly just gags and like games to play, like crossword puzzles and other like pranks you can pull on your friends and stuff like that.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:31.940
So I imagine, you know, I got the general gist of what it was like to grow up with Mad Magazine via Nick Magazine.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.100
Yeah, it's just a much more youth friendly version.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:44.320
Right. I remember in Nickelodeon Magazine that they would have little sections that they had to designate as real.

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:49.600
Nickelodeon Magazine was the first time I actually found out that bed bugs were real.

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:53.460
Like I thought they were just pulling my leg with that don't let the bed bugs bite.

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:59.240
But then Nick Magazine did a whole special on it and like what bed bugs are and what they look like.

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.440
And I was like, what are you kidding me?

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:02.700
Like these things are real?

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:07.660
And I read that at a dentist office.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.240
So that's where most people read their magazines, right?

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:12.520
Nobody actually gets these things delivered to their house.

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:18.080
Yeah, I feel like, you know, if I ever picked up a Mad Magazine, it would have been at a dentist or a doctor's office.

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:20.560
I don't know if I, I never, definitely never had that to my house.

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:26.540
I can't say that about Mad Magazine, but I definitely got Nick Magazine delivered to my house.

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:28.600
And I don't know why you're trying to shame me for it.

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:30.180
I think that's just very bougie.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:34.760
Also, Mike, you sort of made it sound like you got Nick Magazine to your house.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:37.200
You said you used to read, you used to go to the dentist like a lot.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:38.460
He went to the dentist a lot.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:40.700
He had cavities in every molar.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:42.780
I had braces for six years.

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:44.240
No cavities.

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:49.820
But braces for six years will give you a lot of visits to the dentist to read up on every issue of Nick Magazine.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:53.100
Anyway, you guys want to talk about Spy vs.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:54.120
Spy? Yeah.

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:55.600
But not the game?

00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:58.480
I'd like to talk about Spy vs.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:05:59.520
Spy, but not the game.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:01.560
Good, because I don't want to talk about the game just yet.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:12.660
I just feel like, you know, these characters are like D-level Looney Tunes characters that like you're just aware of their existence, but they were never quite funny.

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:17.500
And they never really like were in the mainstream, but you're just aware of their existence.

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:19.500
Does that feel right? You're dealing with the disrespect.

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:21.760
No, I kind of feel the same way though.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:25.100
I never, I never like experienced a Spy vs.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:27.720
Spy story. And you know, I kind of feel the same way.

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:34.440
These remind me of any character in the Pink Panther cartoons that's not the Pink Panther are giving me the same kind of feeling.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:38.040
Oh, yeah. Like there's like the bad, bad guy that like I see and I'm like, who's that guy again?

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:42.780
These guys, I'm like, yeah, I've always seen these guys around, but I never really experienced a story.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:44.180
This is my first Spy vs.

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:45.400
Spy story I've experienced.

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:51.140
Yeah. Pink Panther I always found boring, but you know, Spy vs.

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:56.000
Spy, like the only reason that it's associated with, like we've been talking about,

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.780
we've talked about Mad magazines because Spy vs.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:06:59.120
Spy ran a Mad magazine.

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:01.120
I don't know if we've clarified that yet.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:02.700
That's important. Yeah.

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:14.680
Um, but no, I mean, they're like one page and they're, they're quick and they're self-contained and they usually have some cartoonish gore and violence.

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:17.160
So I always found them funny.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:20.840
Like, you know, some of the, the, the gags that they had were good.

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:28.440
I mean, I can't say that every single one was great, but I'm also not the authority on Spy vs.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:31.720
Spy. I thought that was our whole thing is that we're not the authority on anything.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:35.420
Like we know so little about everything that that makes us qualified to talk about it.

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:38.220
That's true. Well, for me, Spy vs.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:40.240
Spy is kind of like Wile E.

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:43.760
Coyote and Roadrunner, except for it's like two Wile E.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:46.120
Coyotes, but with Roadrunner brains.

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:47.380
Like, does that make sense?

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:49.120
Like, yeah, that kind of makes sense.

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:52.100
Uh, you know, and so that, that could be amusing.

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:54.280
I just never found it to be.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:57.700
That's too bad. Anyway, it's time we talk about the game.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:01.740
Uh, cause it's a, it's a little different and it's a, it's a little hard to describe.

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:06.620
So I'm going to start off talking about what I think an overview of this game is.

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:09.320
And then you guys will tell me how this game really is.

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:11.160
Sound fair? Sounds great.

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:19.420
All right. So in my opinion, the object of the game, you got to collect like various items throughout

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:21.620
the, uh, you're in like this base.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:25.960
Let's say, you know, and it's a bunch of different rooms and you're going through, uh, the dressers

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:28.440
and the closets and all that to try to find things.

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:32.960
And you might find like a briefcase or a key or a bomb.

00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:37.800
Uh, and meanwhile, uh, the other spy, let's say you're the white spy.

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:44.760
Then the black spy, uh, is also in real time as you going throughout these same rooms and

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:47.240
looking for clues and also setting up traps.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:55.120
The idea is, is that before, um, before the opposing player finds the key and the briefcase.

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:59.260
And I think there's maybe one other thing they need, but before they find those objects and

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:03.660
escape the room, you have to escape the room by doing those same things faster.

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:07.520
Uh, I think that's about as much as I have, except for that.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:13.480
There's also like a time limit and that the size of the buildings are dependent on the

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:14.620
difficulty you're playing on.

00:09:14.780 --> 00:09:18.180
What do you guys think? Yeah, I can, I'll, I think that was pretty accurate.

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:20.600
I'd like to expound on it a little bit here though.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:26.820
Uh, that one thing that I thought was interesting is that the game encourages you because it's

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:27.820
a split screen the whole time.

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:31.180
So you, whether you're playing another, another real person or you're playing the computer,

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:35.240
you can see the other character and the game encourages you to like screen peak.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:38.820
Like you're supposed to watch the other character to see where they're planting traps.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:44.440
And then, yeah, you're trying to get these, these four essential items and the briefcase

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:46.460
to carry them in before the other guy gets them.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:48.780
Um, so I just thought that was an interesting thing.

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:52.920
First of all, that it's, it's really the first, like, is it the first split screen game

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:54.880
we've played? I want to say yes.

00:09:54.880 --> 00:10:00.960
And then as an early split screen game, it's, it's like, it's not even a, it doesn't even

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:03.620
have the idea of like, well, you're not supposed to look at the other player's screen.

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:04.940
It's just like, nope, they're both on the screen.

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:07.140
Part of the game is watching the other player.

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:15.040
Yeah. I like also to clarify that there's only one of each of the items that you need that

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:16.160
like, yeah, there's a briefcase.

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:18.760
There's a, there's a bag of money.

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:24.260
There's, uh, like a document and one other thing, I think a key.

00:10:24.460 --> 00:10:27.080
Yeah. And you have to get, there's only one of each.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.320
You have to gather all of them before you can leave.

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:37.660
So it encourages you to, uh, either, uh, spring a trap on the other spy or to just beat them

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:43.240
up. Uh, if you find yourselves in the same room, uh, to take, to take the stuff so that you

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:50.360
can get out. So, and, and yeah, like that, that whole split screen thing, uh, really adds like, especially

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:55.480
if you're playing with another person, cause I don't really know how the AI takes into account,

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:58.260
whether it sees you planting a trap or not.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:06.400
Um, but it's, it's a really unique, uh, it's got a lot of unique gameplay mechanics in this

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:11.980
thing. You know, I think we should talk about that simulplay a little more because, you know,

00:11:12.020 --> 00:11:15.240
this is something we haven't seen in any of the NES games so far.

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:21.960
And it's probably, you know, still an interesting feature today to see in an NES game where the

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:27.240
things that you do on the screen affect the other computer and other player, no matter

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:28.960
where they are in the same building.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:31.500
You know, like Sean said, there's limited resources.

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:34.020
If you're in the same room, you have to fight.

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:39.040
If you plant traps, you have to remember where those traps are planted for yourself, but also

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:42.140
for the other spy, if you watch them plant something.

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:45.800
So there's a lot going on there, but how did you guys feel about the execution of that

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:53.340
simulplay? Uh, initially I was a little, I was a little frustrated because it did feel like some of,

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:58.140
it did feel like some of where the traps are and stuff was a little luck based.

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:01.020
Cause like, you know, you know that there's a trap in a room, but you can't tell which

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:02.140
piece of furniture it's in.

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:04.380
And you also know there's an item you need in there.

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:06.840
Cause your map has indicators on where the items you need are.

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:09.180
So you kind of just got to guess one.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:12.760
But the more I played it, the more I kind of got the hang of like trying to keep an eye

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:17.880
on where the player at the bottom of the screen was planting his bombs, remembering where

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:24.300
things are. And I kind of developed a strategy to like get to the essential items as quickly as possible

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:25.780
before there was time to plant bombs.

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:28.520
And I, so the more I played it, the more I realized that that's kind of part of the

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:33.700
game. I mean, the part of the game is that it is going to be luck based unless you can split

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:36.600
your attention and pay attention to the, to the character below.

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:40.320
Yeah. And, and as I started to kind of get the hang of that, I started to like it a lot more.

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:41.600
Yeah. Cause it really is.

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:47.560
I mean, there are some bombs that are like pre planted, I think, uh, both throughout the

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:51.820
levels. But if you're paying attention, nothing is luck based besides that.

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:57.440
Uh, because you should be able to, uh, play and watch at the same time.

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:06.400
Um, I think it like some of the, but in terms of how in, in execution, these, uh, these elements

00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:09.600
come together, I think it's a, it's a much better idea on paper.

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:16.400
Um, I, I still, I still enjoyed it, but I think that it's, it sounds quite a bit more,

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:21.560
uh, engaging when it's described to you than when you're actually doing it.

