00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:55.540
like the trap situation works, I was a little surprised to find that it's just kind of you

00:30:55.540 --> 00:31:00.640
versus the other spy in like a police baton brutality face off.

00:31:00.640 --> 00:31:06.420
So, I mean, yeah, there's very little depth to the combat itself, but I think it's actually

00:31:06.420 --> 00:31:11.740
doing and I may be giving too much credit to the game is like, yeah, you're just button

00:31:11.740 --> 00:31:19.300
mashing. But because you can't like clip through, you can't clip through the other person.

00:31:19.300 --> 00:31:28.920
And because it takes so many hits when you're unarmed to kill the other person, it's sort

00:31:28.920 --> 00:31:35.820
of like you have a little bit of time to make that decision of whether you think you can take

00:31:35.820 --> 00:31:38.900
care of this and just kill them right here and there.

00:31:38.900 --> 00:31:46.400
Or if you want to flee and think of a different way to dispatch your foe.

00:31:47.240 --> 00:31:54.740
So, I mean, the combat itself isn't very fun, but I think the way that it's designed allows

00:31:54.740 --> 00:31:58.860
you to make different decisions of how you want to go about it.

00:31:59.400 --> 00:32:01.360
Yeah, I completely agree with this.

00:32:01.460 --> 00:32:04.980
And actually, I think, Mike, you kind of said something about this earlier about it being

00:32:04.980 --> 00:32:06.340
like a risk versus reward thing.

00:32:06.340 --> 00:32:12.120
Like there were a lot of times where I'm just trying to make my opponent's timer run out

00:32:12.120 --> 00:32:13.700
quicker. So I'm like, oh, I'm going to fight him.

