00:46:19.760 --> 00:46:23.980
where one is that some of the inputs are kind of weird, strange decisions,

00:46:25.220 --> 00:46:29.680
and are definitely not, like, the ideal gameplay inputs.

00:46:30.360 --> 00:46:34.280
And the other is that playing alone, it can get repetitive.

00:46:34.760 --> 00:46:38.580
I mean, eventually you're just kind of doing the same thing over and over again, granted there's strategy to it,

00:46:38.660 --> 00:46:45.880
but I do think that even with that repetitiveness, it has a way of kind of keeping itself somewhat fresh.

00:46:46.740 --> 00:46:51.700
And playing with a second player, I think, would completely get rid of the feeling of repetitiveness.

00:46:51.760 --> 00:46:57.020
It would be repetitive in the same way that, like, checkers is repetitive, where, yeah, it's just a competition.

00:46:57.020 --> 00:47:02.160
So it's, you know, it's the same thing, but you're trying different things, and you're trying to, you know, beat your opponent.

00:47:03.480 --> 00:47:09.180
So for a while, while I was playing it, I was playing around with this, my patented golden play it idea,

00:47:09.820 --> 00:47:14.120
but the more I got into it, like, honestly, this game got better and better as I played it.

00:47:14.120 --> 00:47:17.680
So I think I'm going to give it an essential vote.

00:47:18.240 --> 00:47:21.260
All right, and that's one, so it just needs one more.

00:47:21.880 --> 00:47:25.120
Sean, will you please give your essential vote?

00:47:25.520 --> 00:47:34.960
Yeah, so I'm always very happy to see unique game mechanics in games.

00:47:34.960 --> 00:47:42.920
And this was the most unique game that we've played in many episodes.

00:47:43.320 --> 00:47:53.820
And while there is some jank, there's certainly some jank, and apparently you can play games like this on other retro systems.

00:47:53.820 --> 00:48:05.220
I think that it's enough of a fresh take on a lot of things.

00:48:05.860 --> 00:48:11.640
And it's not like this hide-and-seek cat-and-mouse thing you don't really get anywhere else.

00:48:11.640 --> 00:48:15.120
I'll have to agree with Joe and actually give this an essential vote.

00:48:15.120 --> 00:48:20.000
All right, and with two votes, that automatically puts it on the essential games list.

00:48:20.160 --> 00:48:25.440
So this is our first one since Life Force to make it on the essential games list.

00:48:26.240 --> 00:48:27.660
That's an unlikely source.

00:48:27.880 --> 00:48:37.340
I know. I will still vote here just to break up the essential votes and make this one not all essential.

00:48:38.600 --> 00:48:42.700
You know, I think I totally understand you guys' reasoning here,

00:48:42.700 --> 00:48:47.940
and it's cool that you can get a game like this that does things different and work out,

00:48:48.140 --> 00:48:51.280
because we've had games recently like Ghostbusters, where it's like,

00:48:51.340 --> 00:48:54.040
huh, that's not where I expect them to take that.

00:48:54.220 --> 00:48:55.720
And then it sucks, you know?

00:48:55.900 --> 00:48:58.200
So it's like, I wish they would have tried the males.

00:48:58.360 --> 00:49:02.100
Here it's like, oh, I could have seen this just being some stupid, like,

00:49:02.720 --> 00:49:09.200
you and another spy on, like, a, you know, a giant grid-like system,

00:49:09.200 --> 00:49:12.780
and you're just hopping around setting up bombs in specific places,

00:49:13.240 --> 00:49:15.880
and, you know, almost like Bomberman, almost, like that kind of style.

00:49:16.100 --> 00:49:17.780
I could see them going that approach.

00:49:18.180 --> 00:49:19.160
And they didn't do that.

00:49:19.280 --> 00:49:21.320
So there's a lot here that I like about it.

00:49:21.420 --> 00:49:26.040
But to be honest, you know, this game personally didn't click a lot for me.

00:49:26.160 --> 00:49:30.180
And I think in today's terms, it doesn't have enough...

00:49:30.180 --> 00:49:34.920
It's a little archaic to learn the systems and stuff like that.

00:49:35.000 --> 00:49:37.040
And I won't say that it's not satisfying once you get it down.

00:49:37.160 --> 00:49:40.140
It's just that in order to have a good time with it,

00:49:40.280 --> 00:49:44.300
you might be forcing yourself to play this game for a little bit.

00:49:44.400 --> 00:49:49.100
And I felt like I was doing this for the show and not out of fun.

00:49:49.240 --> 00:49:53.040
So I'm giving it a play it, but not a essential vote.

00:49:53.720 --> 00:49:55.180
And Mike, I would...

00:49:55.780 --> 00:49:57.860
Because I felt that way when I first started playing it,

00:49:57.860 --> 00:50:01.080
I would still recommend you give it another chance.

00:50:01.660 --> 00:50:05.960
Because I feel like it just takes a long time to really get into it.

00:50:06.040 --> 00:50:08.060
And it just strikes me as something you would like

00:50:08.060 --> 00:50:09.760
if you really got into the weeds of it.

00:50:09.900 --> 00:50:11.440
And I think I definitely will try it again.

00:50:11.620 --> 00:50:13.740
You know, hey, we're going to be playing these essential games

00:50:13.740 --> 00:50:14.880
for a lot longer, right?

00:50:15.580 --> 00:50:17.160
True. Exactly.

00:50:18.020 --> 00:50:19.480
And me and Joe are going to play

00:50:19.860 --> 00:50:22.180
as soon as we see each other in real life again.

00:50:22.320 --> 00:50:23.300
Yeah, in three years.

00:50:25.860 --> 00:50:28.780
Yeah, I think that's the part I'm also curious of, like,

00:50:28.920 --> 00:50:31.020
probably why I'm holding out is just to, like,

00:50:31.380 --> 00:50:32.320
to play with other people.

00:50:32.320 --> 00:50:35.700
That's probably part of the essential experience, you know?

00:50:36.120 --> 00:50:37.620
Yeah, I think it probably is, too.

00:50:37.840 --> 00:50:40.400
Is it weird for me to say that I almost feel this is...

00:50:40.400 --> 00:50:41.300
This is maybe a little out there,

00:50:41.360 --> 00:50:43.740
but I feel like this almost scratches the same kind of itch

00:50:43.740 --> 00:50:46.600
that, like, 007 Golden Eye scratches.

00:50:46.880 --> 00:50:49.300
There's just something about, like, going through these rooms,

00:50:49.420 --> 00:50:52.460
knowing your opponent is somewhere, trying to, like...

00:50:52.460 --> 00:50:55.680
And also screen-looking, because we all know you did that, too.

00:50:56.060 --> 00:50:58.940
I would agree with that if only for the same reasons

00:50:58.940 --> 00:51:01.440
that you don't play Golden Eye today.

00:51:01.740 --> 00:51:05.240
It's that the game is now very archaic and not all that fun.

00:51:05.340 --> 00:51:07.400
Like, you know, you remember it a certain way,

00:51:07.560 --> 00:51:09.820
and that's it. Like, we have no nostalgia for these games.

00:51:10.360 --> 00:51:13.820
I should say my memory of Golden Eye...

00:51:13.820 --> 00:51:16.840
Granted, yes, there are control issues and stuff with Golden Eye,

00:51:16.940 --> 00:51:20.740
but just the core of the way I remember playing Golden Eye

00:51:20.740 --> 00:51:23.140
is just, like, the way I would play a more modern...

00:51:23.880 --> 00:51:26.540
Sure. It's just, for me, both this and Golden Eye,

00:51:26.680 --> 00:51:28.940
like, the way that they play out today,

00:51:29.900 --> 00:51:31.200
you know, leave...

00:51:31.200 --> 00:51:33.000
Like, on paper, they're great games,

00:51:33.020 --> 00:51:35.140
and in the past, they were probably great games,

00:51:35.180 --> 00:51:37.540
but the systems have evolved beyond them, you know?

00:51:37.600 --> 00:51:41.860
It's like, if we never moved up from using the four C buttons

00:51:41.860 --> 00:51:45.820
to control directional movement on controllers,

00:51:46.240 --> 00:51:47.720
then Golden Eye would still hold up,

00:51:47.860 --> 00:51:50.180
but now we have, like, you know,

00:51:50.280 --> 00:51:53.160
a second analog camera that acts as the camera,

00:51:53.260 --> 00:51:56.040
so, you know, it makes it harder to play it today.

00:51:56.240 --> 00:51:58.120
Yeah, but I do think that the, like,

00:51:58.460 --> 00:51:59.440
things that went...

00:51:59.440 --> 00:52:02.080
that look worse now about Golden Eye

00:52:02.780 --> 00:52:04.680
would, like, today feel a lot worse

00:52:04.680 --> 00:52:06.740
than the things that are bad about Spy vs.

00:52:06.860 --> 00:52:09.240
Spy, where it's, like, little input things that are weird,

00:52:09.340 --> 00:52:10.480
but, like, once you get the hang of it,

00:52:10.580 --> 00:52:12.400
it doesn't feel, like, counter to the way

00:52:12.400 --> 00:52:14.460
you've been playing video games for the past 10 years.

00:52:14.940 --> 00:52:16.400
Or, granted, I haven't played Golden Eye in a long time,

00:52:16.500 --> 00:52:17.500
but I imagine if I picked that up,

00:52:17.580 --> 00:52:18.980
I'd be like, what the heck are these controls?

00:52:19.260 --> 00:52:20.360
Like, that's so different.

00:52:20.560 --> 00:52:22.440
Since Spy vs. Spy is just something that's, like,

00:52:22.480 --> 00:52:23.820
I've never played anything else like it

00:52:23.820 --> 00:52:26.320
to, like, train me into thinking a new way

00:52:26.320 --> 00:52:28.540
about playing it like I have with first-person shooters,

00:52:29.340 --> 00:52:31.580
I don't think it's quite as much of a...

00:52:32.060 --> 00:52:34.580
It doesn't hurt as much to, like,

00:52:34.940 --> 00:52:35.820
have to relearn that.

00:52:36.000 --> 00:52:38.320
All right. I mean, you guys have sold

00:52:38.760 --> 00:52:40.280
